nodecode-memos is a rewrite of Markgatcha's Markgatcha/memos:

  https://github.com/Markgatcha/memos
  commit af8fa31e01078e4d5e38210b61acdf90f6d70581
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Markgatcha (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin is a Hermes Agent plugin -- an MCP server
entry (.mcp.json, npx @mem-os/sdk mcp), two session hooks and the /mem-os and
/recall commands -- and this cell is the same surface as a Nodecode cell in
Common Lisp. The six memos_* verbs it exposes are the original's (store,
search, context_pack, supersede, forget, history); /memos stands in for
/mem-os and /recall, with the diagnostics of /mem-os folded into its status
line; (help :memos) is the skill the plugin left to the host; and the
SessionStart summary is the memos section's `standing` setting, off by
default. The facts live in one JSON-lines file under the home (~/.nodecode/
memos/memories.jsonl unless the section names another), appended one line a
store, a supersede or a forget, and the history is the fold of those lines --
the original's SQLite version chain kept as readable data. Nothing is bundled:
no Node, no npm, no @mem-os/sdk, and no vector store.

What was left out, and why: SQLite (this image keeps its own store, and a
bundler for a Node module would be the plugin itself), embeddings and
semantic search (they need a model and a vector index; ranking here is by the
query's terms, its tags, its type and namespace, and the whole phrase, which
needs nothing and never leaves the machine), automatic turn capture and the
SessionEnd consolidation hook (a turn's text is not a fact, and the
original's own skill tells the model to store deliberately), and
memos_diagnostics as a tool (its counts are on /memos). The argument names
are this cell's -- memory_id, memory_type, tokens -- where the original used
id, type and tokenBudget; the verbs, the types, the [mem:xxxxxxxx] ids and
what a stored fact means are the same.
